{
  "teamId": 114,
  "year": 2026,
  "narrative": "The 2026 Cleveland Guardians opened the year with a split personality, playing .500 baseball through March and April while their bullpen — particularly Cade Smith — gave back leads at the worst possible moments. The season's early identity was forged in those painful late-inning surrenders, yet it was also in April that the heroes began to emerge: Daniel Schneemann's grand slam, Brayan Rocchio's go-ahead blast, and Chase DeLauter's clutch extra-base hits signaled that this roster had genuine weapons.\n\nMay became the Guardians' best month, a stretch of 18-11 ball where Kyle Manzardo's big home run and a locked-in rotation gave Cleveland its first real taste of what this team could be. But the momentum stalled hard in June, when the bullpen unraveled again — Cade Smith's catastrophic single that nearly cost a playoff spot, and a disastrous three-game stretch against Houston — dragging the club to 10-15 and raising real questions about whether Stephen Vogt's group could hold it together. The summer months of July and August were a grind, with Brayan Rocchio's two-run walk-off homer on July 2nd standing as the emotional high point of an otherwise exhausting stretch of .500 baseball.\n\nSeptember saved everything. A 16-9 surge driven by Rocchio's stunning late-season double, Petey Halpin's clutch three-run blast, and Angel Martínez's two-run shot pushed Cleveland into the postseason at 85-77. Rocchio in particular emerged as the team's most important player down the stretch, his bat appearing in nearly every pivotal moment. The Guardians had done what Guardians teams do — survive long enough to make it matter in October.\n\nNow Cleveland finds itself knotted at two games apiece in the ALDS, a series that has delivered everything the regular season promised: gut-punch moments from the opposition, clutch answers from Rocchio, Ramírez, and the newly vital Jo Adell, whose back-to-back postseason triples have given the lineup an unexpected lift. With the series tied and the pressure at its peak, the 2026 Guardians are right where they've lived all year — one big moment from moving on, one bad inning from going home.",
